WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.482 --> 00:00:09.499
Hi, my name is Sean Fredella.

00:00:09.499 --> 00:00:14.542
If you don't know me, I hope
you never meet somebody like me again.

00:00:14.542 --> 00:00:18.363
That's because I've survived cancer
four times.

00:00:18.363 --> 00:00:22.091
Four times over the course of
seven and a half years.

00:00:22.091 --> 00:00:23.640
I've had Leukemia,

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.338
a bone marrow transplant,

00:00:25.338 --> 00:00:26.633
chemo,

00:00:26.633 --> 00:00:28.182
radiation.

00:00:28.182 --> 00:00:29.691
I've had a craniotomy,

00:00:29.691 --> 00:00:32.980
which is basically a fancy term 
that doctors use

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:36.980
for cutting my head open
from this ear to this ear,

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:38.849
pulling down my face,

00:00:38.849 --> 00:00:43.439
then use a drill to pull out the tumor
the size of a golf ball.

00:00:43.439 --> 00:00:46.732
Yeah, fifth grade was not very fun.

00:00:46.732 --> 00:00:49.063
I had many memories of cancer.

00:00:49.063 --> 00:00:51.671
Some good, some bad.

00:00:51.671 --> 00:00:54.034
I remember my family visiting me,

00:00:54.034 --> 00:00:58.980
cousins, all my aunts and uncles,
having good times,

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:02.437
watching movies, pulling all-nighters,

00:01:02.437 --> 00:01:06.851
maybe being the most competitive
bingo player ever.

00:01:06.851 --> 00:01:11.747
But some of my most memorable
moments were with steroids.

00:01:11.747 --> 00:01:16.691
Now, when I say steroids, most people
think of steroids like Barry Bonds,

00:01:16.691 --> 00:01:20.013
get ripped, muscular,

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:27.464
or like Lance Armstrong,
or every single biker ever.

00:01:27.464 --> 00:01:29.362
No, I had a different type,

00:01:29.362 --> 00:01:39.450
and to put it lightly, this steroid
made me angry.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:45.026
I was mad all the time and I complained
about just about everything.

00:01:45.026 --> 00:01:58.514
I had the temper of 1,000 Donald Trumps.

00:01:58.514 --> 00:02:01.362
Now, one time during Lent,

00:02:01.362 --> 00:02:06.913
after church, I was walking outside
and an old lady started to approach me,

00:02:06.913 --> 00:02:08.483
and she was shaking.

00:02:08.483 --> 00:02:12.812
She was more nervous
than me giving a speech right now.

00:02:12.812 --> 00:02:21.233
And she said, "Now Sean, what
are you giving up for Lent this year?"

00:02:21.233 --> 00:02:24.276
I looked her straight in the eyes
and I said,

00:02:24.276 --> 00:02:28.903
"Duh, my hair."

00:02:28.903 --> 00:02:33.038
Now, all jokes aside, cancer was
a challenge for me.

00:02:33.038 --> 00:02:35.804
I remember being held down
by four to five nurses,

00:02:35.804 --> 00:02:38.307
getting needles in my chest,

00:02:38.307 --> 00:02:41.957
which is, by the way,
way scarier than public speaking.

00:02:41.957 --> 00:02:44.456
And I met lots of friends.

00:02:44.456 --> 00:02:45.824
We fought with each other

00:02:45.824 --> 00:02:49.998
and most of my friends didn't make it.

00:02:49.998 --> 00:02:53.324
One of my friend's name was Tyler.

00:02:53.324 --> 00:02:58.227
One day, my dad took Tyler and me
to go see a movie.

00:02:58.227 --> 00:03:02.895
Tyler was 14 years old
and he just had a relapse.

00:03:02.895 --> 00:03:04.718
Tyler said to my dad,

00:03:04.718 --> 00:03:09.705
"Mr. Fredella, all I want to do
is drive a car."

00:03:09.705 --> 00:03:14.028
Tyler passed away on March 15, 2012.

00:03:14.028 --> 00:03:17.148
He was 14 years old.

00:03:17.148 --> 00:03:20.365
He was never able to drive a car.

00:03:20.365 --> 00:03:24.360
It's moments like these
and people like Tyler

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.426
that make me wonder why.

00:03:26.426 --> 00:03:27.876
Why me?

00:03:27.876 --> 00:03:31.394
Out of all my friends, why did I survive?

00:03:31.394 --> 00:03:33.528
There has to be a reason.

00:03:33.528 --> 00:03:37.795
And I think I know an answer,
and I'd like to share it with you.

00:03:37.795 --> 00:03:41.030
I believe it's my purpose 
to raise awareness

00:03:41.030 --> 00:03:44.549
about the money allocated
to pediatric cancer research.

00:03:44.549 --> 00:03:47.477
You know, out of all the money raised,

00:03:47.477 --> 00:03:52.249
the millions, billions of dollars
raised to fight all cancers,

00:03:52.249 --> 00:03:58.646
pediatric cancer only gets 4%.

00:03:58.646 --> 00:04:00.589
Kids like you,

00:04:00.589 --> 00:04:02.337
kids like me,

00:04:02.337 --> 00:04:04.347
and kids like Tyler,

00:04:04.347 --> 00:04:08.100
and it's only 4%.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:14.568
Children die every day from cancer,
and they did nothing to deserve it.

00:04:14.568 --> 00:04:16.301
We need to fight.

00:04:16.301 --> 00:04:18.331
We need to make a change.

00:04:18.331 --> 00:04:21.571
We need to have more than four.

00:04:21.571 --> 00:04:25.410
And I think I can fix it,
I think I have a solution.

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:29.550
Well, here it goes.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:33.731
Ladies and gentlemen, sports!

00:04:33.731 --> 00:04:38.862
In America, we worship the athletes
who walk the hardwood courts

00:04:38.862 --> 00:04:40.841
and awesome fields.

00:04:40.841 --> 00:04:43.001
We wear Jordans because they wear Jordans.

00:04:43.001 --> 00:04:47.282
We buy jerseys with their names on them
because we want to be like them.

00:04:47.282 --> 00:04:51.250
Now, when you look at this picture,
what do you notice?

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:54.440
What stands out?

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:59.917
Now does anyone know what pink stands for?

00:04:59.917 --> 00:05:04.929
When October rolls around,
the whole sports world is painted pink.

00:05:04.929 --> 00:05:09.175
Everyone comes together
for a great cause and a great message

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:15.516
that helps breast cancer survivors
and patients fight breast cancer.

00:05:15.516 --> 00:05:20.250
Now, just like breast cancer
uses pink as their color,

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:25.490
we could use gold, which is the color
for pediatric cancer awareness.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:27.620
Gold on gloves,

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:29.901
gold on cleats,

00:05:29.901 --> 00:05:32.500
gold on clothes.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:35.033
We can fight pediatric cancer.

00:05:35.033 --> 00:05:36.454
I can see it now.

00:05:36.454 --> 00:05:39.980
It's the first Sunday of the NFL season.

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:45.043
100,000 fans chanting,
anticipating a great kick-off.

00:05:45.043 --> 00:05:48.850
And out of the tunnels,
out of the corners of the stadium,

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:52.446
a blur of gold streaks across the field.

00:05:52.446 --> 00:05:54.544
Thousands of fans chanting,

00:05:54.544 --> 00:06:00.301
and the gold gloves and cleats
glisten in the Sunday morning sun.

00:06:00.301 --> 00:06:07.901
All players from different places
with different dreams and different homes

00:06:07.901 --> 00:06:14.896
come together for one cause
and one color - gold.

00:06:14.896 --> 00:06:17.411
We need to fight pedatric cancer,

00:06:17.411 --> 00:06:21.075
and I believe that 
this is the way to do it.

00:06:21.075 --> 00:06:23.655
From peewee to professional,

00:06:23.655 --> 00:06:26.046
from kickers to coaches,

00:06:26.046 --> 00:06:28.362
from goalies to guards,

00:06:28.362 --> 00:06:31.512
everyone can wear gold in September.

00:06:31.512 --> 00:06:33.922
My goal is that by this time next year,

00:06:33.922 --> 00:06:37.892
everybody knows that September
is the month for raising awareness

00:06:37.892 --> 00:06:39.613
for childhood cancer research,

00:06:39.613 --> 00:06:45.253
and that gold is the color
for finding a cure for childhood cancer.

00:06:45.253 --> 00:06:48.506
Now, don't take my word for it.

00:06:48.506 --> 00:06:53.713
Who am I? Just a random kid
from Mountain Brook, Alabama.

00:06:53.713 --> 00:06:58.486
Why don't you take the number three 
pick in the 2016 NFL draft

00:06:58.486 --> 00:07:00.668
Joey Bosa's word for it.

00:07:00.668 --> 00:07:04.712
I'm Joey Bosa former defensive end
at the Ohio State University

00:07:04.712 --> 00:07:07.977
and I believe our children 
deserve more than four.

00:07:07.977 --> 00:07:11.964
And why don't you take defensive lineman
from Iowa State University

00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:13.938
Mitchell Myers's word for it.

00:07:13.938 --> 00:07:18.342
Hi, my name is Mitchell Myers.
I play football at Iowa State University,

00:07:18.342 --> 00:07:20.076
and I'm a cancer survivor.

00:07:20.076 --> 00:07:23.062
Our kids deserve more than four.

00:07:23.062 --> 00:07:25.887
And, since we're in New York,

00:07:25.887 --> 00:07:30.178
why don't you take arguably the greatest
New York Yankees' pitcher ever,

00:07:30.178 --> 00:07:31.887
Andy Pettitte.

00:07:31.887 --> 00:07:35.555
Hi, this Andy Pettitte,
former pitcher of the New York Yankees

00:07:35.555 --> 00:07:37.070
and Houston Astros.

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:39.957
Our kids deserve more than four.

00:07:39.957 --> 00:07:42.408
Hi. My name is Sean Fredella.

00:07:42.408 --> 00:07:45.399
Our kids deserve more than four.

00:07:45.399 --> 00:07:48.028
Tyler deserves more than four.

00:07:48.028 --> 00:07:51.895
Sebastian deserves more than four.

00:07:51.895 --> 00:07:54.607
Andrew deserves more than four.

00:07:54.607 --> 00:07:56.875
We deserve more than four.

00:07:56.875 --> 00:07:57.729
Thank you.

